static const profile_guide_block_t profile_guide_blocks_13[] = {
  {PG_TABLE,"Symptom\tCheck\nA setting seems ignored\tExact key/section spelling, one source block, no inline value comments, and latest patch installed.\nGUI changes disappear on a new run\tAssemble & Emulate reapplies explicit source settings. Edit the source.\nA supposedly disabled device appears\tDisable it explicitly; omitted values can inherit a saved profile.\nVIA2 does not operate the LCD/SD\tVIA2 enabled, 2... pin strings, correct driver base, correct DDR settings.\nLCD is enabled but blank\tLCD initialisation sequence, correct wiring/width, DDR registers and command delays.\nSimple console has no input\tRX enabled at TX+1, simulator running, console focused, code reading RX.\nACIA receives nothing\tCorrect transport/port, TCP client connected or physical Connect pressed, matching ACIA registers and driver base.\nW65C51N loses burst output\tCheck acia_tx_bug; the enabled fault requires software pacing.\nSD shows no card\tExisting image, correct source-relative path, compatible geometry and permissions.\nSD reads work but writes fail\tread_only, image permissions, driver errors and strict timing limits.\nSD initialisation fails only in strict mode\tAt least 74 startup clocks with CS high, CS handling and SPI frequency no greater than the startup limit.\nTwo peripherals interfere\tOverlapping CPU address ranges or shared VIA pins/direction changes."},
  {PG_TEXT,"This guide was checked against the patched profile parser and GTK application paths. Its device snippets describe configuration; they are not complete driver programs. Hardware-model limitations still apply."},
};
static const profile_guide_section_t profile_guide_sections[] = {
  {"Overview",profile_guide_blocks_0,3,"Overview\n\nThis guide describes the source-profile format in the ACIA + SD source profiles patch. Apply that patch and rebuild before using the new ACIA fields or SD read_only option.\n\nA profile describes the simulated machine: its memory map, enabled peripherals, wiring and device options. Your assembly program still initialises and operates those devices.\n\nOpen Help → Source Profile Guide in the GTK editor. Select a section on the left or search its title, explanation, settings and examples. Multiple search words must all occur in the section. Use Copy example to copy a code block unchanged, or Copy section for the entire entry. Ctrl+F focuses search; Escape closes the guide. The built-in guide works offline and is included in the binary."},
  {"Writing and using a source profile",profile_guide_blocks_1,13,"Writing and using a source profile\n\nPlace one complete profile block near the beginning of the main assembly source file. The opening marker also starts the [profile] section:\n\n;[profile]\n;name = My machine\n;cpu = 6502\n;[io]\n;simio_tx_enable = 1\n;simio_tx_addr = 9030\n;simio_rx_enable = 1\n;simio_rx_addr = 9031\n;[/profile]\n\nUse Assembly → Assemble & Emulate to apply it. The editor extracts the commented settings and opens the simulator with that configuration. These comments do not emit machine code. A CLI assembly alone does not launch this GUI workflow. The examples in individual device sections below are fragments to place inside the same block; the combined example supplies the complete block.\n\nSyntax rules\n\n• Begin with exactly ;[profile] and end with ;[/profile].\n• Prefix every section header and setting with ;.\n• Use lower-case section names and keys as shown.\n• Write one key = value per line. Do not put explanatory comments after values.\n• Use 0 for disabled and 1 for enabled.\n• Write addresses and memory sizes in hexadecimal: 9000, C000, 4000. Do not use the assembly $ prefix in profile values.\n• Write timing values, SPI frequency, TCP ports, bit numbers, bank numbers and display dimensions in decimal.\n• Write names and paths without quotation marks. Spaces within a path are allowed.\n• Avoid repeating a key. Where accepted repeatedly, later values take precedence.\n• Put an LCD’s bus_width before its data-pin entries.\n\nFor explanation within the block, a hash-comment line works:\n\n;# LCD on VIA1; SD card on VIA2\n\nUnrecognised keys are generally ignored rather than reported as assembly errors. A successful assembly therefore does not prove that every profile setting applied.\n\nInheritance and saved settings\n\nThe editor starts with the last-selected/default saved machine profile and then applies the settings in the source block. If no saved profile is available, it uses built-in profile defaults. Omitted fields retain those inherited values. Explicitly disable unused devices when distributing a self-contained example.\n\nACIA model, fault and connection fields fall back to saved sim.cfg preferences if neither the source nor the base machine profile specifies them. SD settings come from the base .simprofile or its built-in defaults.\n\nLoading a source profile does not rewrite the saved profile or save its ACIA model/connection overrides as your personal defaults. Deliberately changing ACIA controls or clicking Connect retains the GUI’s normal preference-saving behavior.\n\nA simulator Reset keeps the active session configuration. A fresh Assemble & Emulate reapplies the source settings, so edit the source block if you want a lasting change to that example."},
  {"CPU and profile identity",profile_guide_blocks_2,6,"CPU and profile identity\n\nThese fields belong directly after the opening marker:\n\n;[profile]\n;name = LCD and SD development board\n;description = Two VIAs, LCD, ACIA and SD card\n;cpu = 6502\n\nKey\tMeaning\nname\tShort machine/profile name.\ndescription\tDescription of its purpose.\ncpu\tParsed values: 6502, 6502u, 65c02, 65816.\nglobal\tSaved-profile scope metadata; normally omit in source examples.\n\nThe profile’s CPU field is separate from the assembler directive:\n\n    .cpu 6502\n\nKeep the assembly directive, profile and simulator CPU selection consistent. In the current GTK launch path, the configured simulator CPU takes precedence when the window is created; the profile field alone is not sufficient to switch CPU execution mode. 65816 simulation is incomplete."},
  {"ROM ranges",profile_guide_blocks_3,7,"ROM ranges\n\nUp to four ranges are available: [rom0], [rom1], [rom2], [rom3]. A typical example maps its program and vectors into the top 16 KiB:\n\n;[rom0]\n;enabled = 1\n;bank = -1\n;from = C000\n;to = FFFF\n;rw_bit = -1\n;lock_bit = -1\n\nKey\tMeaning\nenabled\tEnable this ROM range.\nbank\t-1 for all banks; use this for ordinary flat-memory examples.\nfrom, to\tInclusive hexadecimal address range.\nrw_bit\tControl-register bit 0–7, or -1 for no control connection.\nlock_bit\tControl-register bit 0–7, or -1 for no lock connection.\n\nThis setting does not assemble or relocate the program. Your code still needs a matching origin and vectors, for example .org $C000 and vectors at $FFFA. The editor loads the assembled image separately.\n\nThe current ROM model keeps a ROM snapshot for reads. In ROM mode, writes go to underlying shadow RAM while reads continue to return ROM contents. In RAM-overlay mode, reads return that underlying RAM and writes are discarded. This is the simulator’s implemented overlay behavior; it is not ordinary writable RAM mode.\n\nDo not place a ROM range across device registers, zero page or the stack in a basic example. Disable inherited ranges you do not intend to use:\n\n;[rom1]\n;enabled = 0\n;[rom2]\n;enabled = 0\n;[rom3]\n;enabled = 0"},
  {"ROM protection control register",profile_guide_blocks_4,5,"ROM protection control register\n\nThis optional register controls the rw_bit and lock_bit connections above. For normal examples, leave it disabled:\n\n;[rom_ctrl]\n;enabled = 0\n;bank = -1\n;addr = 7FFF\n\nFor an overlay experiment, enable it and assign separate control bits:\n\n;[rom0]\n;enabled = 1\n;bank = -1\n;from = C000\n;to = FFFF\n;rw_bit = 0\n;lock_bit = 1\n;[rom_ctrl]\n;enabled = 1\n;bank = -1\n;addr = 7FFF\n\nA write to $7FFF uses bit 0 to select that range’s read source: 0 = ROM, 1 = shadow RAM. Setting bit 1 locks the range’s control state until reset. Use bits 0–7 only, avoid accidental sharing between ranges, and keep this register outside the ROM/device regions it must coexist with."},
  {"VIA1",profile_guide_blocks_5,6,"VIA1\n\nVIA1 occupies 16 registers starting at its base address:\n\n;[io]\n;via1_enable = 1\n;via1_bank = -1\n;via1_addr = 9000\n\nThis maps VIA1 to $9000–$900F. Useful register offsets are:\n\nRegister\tOffset\tWith base $9000\nPort B\t+0\t$9000\nPort A, handshake register\t+1\t$9001\nData-direction register B\t+2\t$9002\nData-direction register A\t+3\t$9003\nTimer 1 counter low/high\t+4, +5\t$9004, $9005\nAuxiliary control register\t+B\t$900B\nPeripheral control register\t+C\t$900C\nInterrupt flag register\t+D\t$900D\nInterrupt enable register\t+E\t$900E\n\nThe profile does not configure data directions, timers, edge detection or interrupt enables. Your code writes those registers. For example, an 8-bit LCD using all of port A and B0–B2 normally needs DDRA = $FF and those three DDRB bits set as outputs.\n\nA pin string such as 1A3 means VIA1, port A, bit 3. Pin strings identify the VIA instance, independently of its register address."},
  {"VIA2",profile_guide_blocks_6,5,"VIA2\n\nVIA2 has the same register layout:\n\n;[io]\n;via2_enable = 1\n;via2_bank = -1\n;via2_addr = 9010\n\nThis maps VIA2 to $9010–$901F. A pin such as 2B3 means VIA2, port B, bit 3. Both the LCD and SD card can use VIA2.\n\nTo move an LCD from VIA1 to VIA2, enable VIA2, change its LCD pin strings from 1... to 2..., and change the assembly driver’s VIA base address to match. Changing only via1_addr to via2_addr does not change the peripheral pin wiring.\n\nGive enabled VIAs distinct, non-overlapping register ranges."},
  {"Simulator console and PC keyboard",profile_guide_blocks_7,7,"Simulator console and PC keyboard\n\nThe simple simulator console is separate from the ACIA. It is convenient for prompts, progress messages and interactive menus without a serial client.\n\n;[io]\n;simio_tx_enable = 1\n;simio_tx_bank = -1\n;simio_tx_addr = 9030\n;simio_rx_enable = 1\n;simio_rx_bank = -1\n;simio_rx_addr = 9031\n\nOperation\tDefault address\tEffect\nCPU write\t$9030\tSends a character to the simulator console.\nCPU read\t$9031\tWaits for a queued keyboard character while running.\n\nFor example:\n\nCONSOLE_TX = $9030\nCONSOLE_RX = $9031\n\n    lda #'>'\n    sta CONSOLE_TX\n    lda CONSOLE_RX\n    sta CONSOLE_TX\n\nFocus the simulator console when entering keys. Keyboard input through this interface does not automatically become ACIA receive data.\n\nKeep TX and RX adjacent, with RX = TX + 1, as shown: the current GUI mapper uses a shared two-byte region. Avoid relying on TX/RX as completely independent hardware channels; the implementation also shares its legacy enable state. To turn the simple console off, disable both fields."},
  {"HD44780 LCD",profile_guide_blocks_8,12,"HD44780 LCD\n\nThe LCD connects through VIA pins; it has no separate CPU memory-map base address.\n\n8-bit wiring example\n\nThis uses VIA1 port A for data and B0–B2 for control:\n\n;[lcd]\n;enabled = 1\n;bus_width = 8\n;cols = 16\n;rows = 2\n;strict = 0\n;rs = 1B0\n;rw = 1B1\n;e = 1B2\n;d0 = 1A0\n;d1 = 1A1\n;d2 = 1A2\n;d3 = 1A3\n;d4 = 1A4\n;d5 = 1A5\n;d6 = 1A6\n;d7 = 1A7\n\n4-bit wiring example\n\nThis connects the LCD’s D4–D7 to VIA1 A4–A7:\n\n;[lcd]\n;enabled = 1\n;bus_width = 4\n;cols = 16\n;rows = 2\n;strict = 0\n;rs = 1B0\n;rw = 1B1\n;e = 1B2\n;d4 = 1A4\n;d5 = 1A5\n;d6 = 1A6\n;d7 = 1A7\n\nKey\tMeaning\nenabled\tEnable LCD emulation/display.\nbus_width\tPhysical connection width: 4 or 8.\ncols, rows\tDisplay geometry; common choices are 16/2 and 20/4.\nstrict\t1 enforces the implemented LCD busy timing; 0 is permissive.\nrs, rw, e\tRegister select, read/write and enable control pins.\nd0–d7\tData pins for an 8-bit connection.\nd4–d7\tData pins for a 4-bit connection.\n\nPut bus_width before the data-pin lines because it affects how the parser stores the mapping. The mapped VIA must also be enabled in [io].\n\nYour program must set VIA directions, send the LCD initialisation sequence and allow sufficient command delays. enabled = 1 does not initialise the controller. For write-only operation, map RW and hold that VIA output low in your driver.\n\nThe connection width and controller mode are related but distinct. With all eight data wires connected, software can switch the controller between 8-bit and 4-bit operation. A profile describing only four wires cannot provide an 8-bit data bus.\n\nDo not assign the same control pins to the LCD and SD card unless you deliberately implement and understand shared wiring. The combined example uses separate VIAs."},
  {"6551 ACIA serial interface",profile_guide_blocks_9,22,"6551 ACIA serial interface\n\nThe ACIA occupies four consecutive registers. Model and connection fields also belong in [io].\n\nGeneric 6551 over TCP\n\n;[io]\n;acia_enable = 1\n;acia_bank = -1\n;acia_addr = 9020\n;acia_model = generic6551\n;acia_tx_bug = 0\n;acia_transport = tcp\n;acia_tcp_port = 6551\n\nRegister\tWith base $9020\nData\t$9020\nStatus on read / reset on write\t$9021\nCommand\t$9022\nControl\t$9023\n\nThe simulator starts its TCP listener when ACIA is enabled. Connect the external serial test client to 127.0.0.1:6551. The program must still initialise the ACIA registers and handle data; typing in the simulator’s simple console does not feed this TCP connection.\n\nW65C51S\n\nUse the same mapping and connection fields, changing:\n\n;acia_model = w65c51s\n;acia_tx_bug = 0\n\nW65C51N and its transmit fault\n\n;acia_model = w65c51n\n;acia_tx_bug = 1\n\nModel\tImplemented behavior\ngeneric6551\tNormal transmit-readiness polling, parity and basic TX IRQ support.\nw65c51s\tNormal readiness, parity and basic TX IRQ support in the current model.\nw65c51n\tWDC N model restrictions, with the transmit fault independently switchable.\n\nWith W65C51N and acia_tx_bug = 1, TDRE reads as ready even while transmitting. An early data write replaces the unfinished byte and restarts its simulated frame timing. Software must pace writes according to baud and frame length, including allowing the final byte time to leave.\n\nWith acia_tx_bug = 0, readiness reads normally and writes while busy are ignored. The chip remains W65C51N: its other implemented restrictions, including no parity and no TX IRQ, remain. The option has no effect on Generic 6551 or W65C51S. The GUI shows the active checkbox and ON/OFF status.\n\nThese are byte-level transport models, not complete electrical models. Early sample/lot-specific faults are excluded. Generic/S do not model a complete separate TDR/shift-register pipeline, and the transport does not reproduce corrupt wire bits.\n\nPhysical serial port\n\nFor Linux:\n\n;acia_transport = serial\n;acia_serial_device = /dev/ttyUSB0\n\nFor Windows:\n\n;acia_transport = serial\n;acia_serial_device = COM3\n\nThe profile populates the connection controls. Click Connect to open the physical port; loading source alone does not open it. Force Disconnect closes an active physical connection, or drops the current TCP client while retaining the listener. It is an action, not a profile key.\n\nConnection key\tValues\nacia_transport\ttcp or serial.\nacia_tcp_port\tDecimal 1–65535; used for TCP.\nacia_serial_device\tOperating-system device path/name; used for serial.\n\nBaud rate, data bits, parity and stop bits come from the emulated ACIA’s registers, not extra profile fields. Keep those register settings compatible with the peer. An OS serial port being open does not prove that a remote device has responded."},
  {"SD card over SPI",profile_guide_blocks_10,28,"SD card over SPI\n\nThe SD card connects through VIA pins and uses an existing raw disk-image file. It does not have a separate CPU address range.\n\nWritable SDSC image on VIA2\n\n;[sd]\n;enabled = 1\n;card_type = sdsc\n;image = cards/test.img\n;read_only = 0\n;strict = 1\n;read_us = 100\n;write_us = 2000\n;erase_us = 10000\n;max_spi_hz = 25000000\n;sck = 2B0\n;mosi = 2B1\n;miso = 2B2\n;cs = 2B3\n\nEnable VIA2 separately in [io]. Your SPI driver must use its matching base address. For this wiring, B0, B1 and B3 are outputs, B2 is input: a simple full-port DDRB value is $0B. Keep CS high when the card is deselected.\n\nKey\tMeaning\nenabled\tEnable the SD peripheral.\ncard_type\tauto, sdsc, or sdhc.\nimage\tExisting raw image path, or empty to specify no inserted card.\nread_only\t1 opens read-only and rejects emulated writes/erase; 0 permits writes.\nstrict\tEnforce implemented startup clocks, SPI limits and operation delays.\nsck\tClock output from VIA.\nmosi\tData output from VIA to card.\nmiso\tData input to VIA from card.\ncs\tActive-low chip select output from VIA.\n\nCard type and filesystem are different choices\n\ncard_type\tAddressing\nsdsc\tByte addressed; supports the implemented variable block-length behavior.\nsdhc\t512-byte block addressed.\nauto\tSDSC through 2 GiB; SDHC above 2 GiB.\n\nA small, suitably aligned image can be forced to SDHC for driver testing. The image still needs a size representable by the selected card model. SDHC images must be multiples of 512 KiB; SDSC capacity is limited to 2 GiB and its supported CSD geometry. Use an image produced by the application’s Create dialog when unsure.\n\nFAT12, FAT16 and FAT32 describe the image’s filesystem, not the card type. There is no [sd] fat = 16 key. Select/create the filesystem separately. The supplied native 6502 FAT16 library needs a valid FAT16 image unless using its explicit formatter.\n\nImage paths and mounting\n\nimage = cards/test.img resolves relative to the main assembly source directory. For /home/mike/demos/menu.asm, it refers to /home/mike/demos/cards/test.img. An ordinary standalone .simprofile instead resolves relative image paths against that profile file’s directory.\n\nAbsolute paths also work:\n\n;image = /home/mike/sd images/test.img\n\nOr on Windows:\n\n;image = C:/6502caEd/cards/test.img\n\nDo not surround the path with quotes. If the file cannot be opened, no card is mounted and the application logs a warning. The profile does not create, format or resize the image.\n\nTo explicitly start with no card:\n\n;[sd]\n;enabled = 1\n;image =\n\nTo disable the peripheral entirely:\n\n;[sd]\n;enabled = 0\n\nThose settings differ: an enabled slot with no card allows no-card testing.\n\nRead-only testing\n\n;[sd]\n;enabled = 1\n;image = cards/reference.img\n;read_only = 1\n\nReads remain available. Guest single/multi-block writes and erase commands are rejected, and reset retains write protection. The SD status shows read-only and the profile editor has a matching checkbox. Use a writable copy when running create/write/format examples.\n\nTiming\n\nField\tUnits\tRepresentative default\tAccepted range\nread_us\tMicroseconds\t100\t0–1000000\nwrite_us\tMicroseconds\t2000\t0–1000000\nerase_us\tMicroseconds\t10000\t0–10000000\nmax_spi_hz\tHz\t25000000\t0–25000000\n\nTiming enforcement requires strict = 1. Startup also requires at least 74 clocks with CS high; before initialisation, a nonzero SPI limit is capped at 400 kHz. With strict mode, excessive clock speed causes transfers to fail until CS is released. max_spi_hz = 0 disables clock-limit checking, including that startup limit. It does not disable the other strict-mode rules.\n\nThese defaults are representative presets, not measured speeds for every SDSC or SDHC card. Delays use simulated CPU cycles, so your bit-banged SPI code and delay routines must account for the selected CPU speed. There is no separate profile key for CPU clock speed or strict initialisation checking in this format."},
  {"Banking fields",profile_guide_blocks_11,4,"Banking fields\n\nThe parser accepts this advanced section:\n\n;[banking]\n;enable = 0\n;bank = -1\n;addr = 7FF0\n;window = 8000\n;window_sz = 4000\n;num_banks = 4\n\nKey\tMeaning\nenable\tEnable bank-register handling; note the singular enable.\nbank\tBank metadata for the control register.\naddr\tHexadecimal bank-register address.\nwindow\tHexadecimal start of banked window.\nwindow_sz\tHexadecimal window size in bytes.\nnum_banks\tDecimal number of banks.\n\nLeave banking disabled for the examples in this guide. Parsing this section does not itself load a banked ROM image. The current GTK path requires a populated banked ROM backing store, and its device mapping callbacks do not generally enforce all parsed per-device bank fields. Treat these fields as advanced/partial support, not a promise of complete banked-machine emulation. Banking is disabled for 65816 in this profile path; this is not a general 24-bit 65816 memory-map configuration."},
  {"Combined profile",profile_guide_blocks_12,13,"Combined profile\n\nThis example combines:\n\n• A flat 6502 program ROM at $C000–$FFFF.\n• VIA1 at $9000, driving an 8-bit 20×4 LCD.\n• VIA2 at $9010, driving the SD card over SPI.\n• Generic 6551 ACIA at $9020, listening on TCP port 6551.\n• Console output at $9030 and PC keyboard input at $9031.\n• Explicitly disabled extra ROM ranges, ROM controls and banking.\n\nThe LCD and SD pins do not overlap:\n\nDevice\tVIA\tPins\nLCD data D0–D7\tVIA1\tA0–A7\nLCD RS / RW / E\tVIA1\tB0 / B1 / B2\nSD SCK / MOSI / MISO / CS\tVIA2\tB0 / B1 / B2 / B3\n\nPaste this single block into the main source:\n\n;[profile]\n;name = LCD ACIA SD development board\n;description = VIA1 LCD, VIA2 SD, ACIA and console\n;cpu = 6502\n;[rom0]\n;enabled = 1\n;bank = -1\n;from = C000\n;to = FFFF\n;rw_bit = -1\n;lock_bit = -1\n;[rom1]\n;enabled = 0\n;[rom2]\n;enabled = 0\n;[rom3]\n;enabled = 0\n;[rom_ctrl]\n;enabled = 0\n;bank = -1\n;addr = 7FFF\n;[io]\n;via1_enable = 1\n;via1_bank = -1\n;via1_addr = 9000\n;via2_enable = 1\n;via2_bank = -1\n;via2_addr = 9010\n;acia_enable = 1\n;acia_bank = -1\n;acia_addr = 9020\n;acia_model = generic6551\n;acia_tx_bug = 0\n;acia_transport = tcp\n;acia_tcp_port = 6551\n;simio_tx_enable = 1\n;simio_tx_bank = -1\n;simio_tx_addr = 9030\n;simio_rx_enable = 1\n;simio_rx_bank = -1\n;simio_rx_addr = 9031\n;[lcd]\n;enabled = 1\n;bus_width = 8\n;cols = 20\n;rows = 4\n;strict = 0\n;rs = 1B0\n;rw = 1B1\n;e = 1B2\n;d0 = 1A0\n;d1 = 1A1\n;d2 = 1A2\n;d3 = 1A3\n;d4 = 1A4\n;d5 = 1A5\n;d6 = 1A6\n;d7 = 1A7\n;[sd]\n;enabled = 1\n;card_type = sdsc\n;image = cards/test.img\n;read_only = 0\n;strict = 1\n;read_us = 100\n;write_us = 2000\n;erase_us = 10000\n;max_spi_hz = 25000000\n;sck = 2B0\n;mosi = 2B1\n;miso = 2B2\n;cs = 2B3\n;[banking]\n;enable = 0\n;bank = -1\n;addr = 7FF0\n;window = 8000\n;window_sz = 4000\n;num_banks = 4\n;[/profile]\n\nMake the program’s constants agree with that map:\n\n    .cpu 6502\n\nVIA1_BASE = $9000\nVIA2_BASE = $9010\nACIA_BASE = $9020\nCONSOLE_TX = $9030\nCONSOLE_RX = $9031\n\n    .org $C000\n\nThese constants are separate from the profile comments. Changing a profile address does not automatically change a driver’s constants or included configuration.\n\nBefore running, supply a supported image at cards/test.img, or change image = to leave the slot empty. Then your program must initialise the VIAs, LCD, ACIA and SD card. Enabling all devices does not run any of their driver routines.\n\nFor the W65C51N transmit test, change only these two lines in the combined block:\n\n;acia_model = w65c51n\n;acia_tx_bug = 1\n\nFor a 4-bit LCD, replace the complete [lcd] section with the 4-bit example, retaining cols = 20 and rows = 4 if required, and use a matching 4-bit driver."},
  {"Troubleshooting",profile_guide_blocks_13,2,"Troubleshooting\n\nSymptom\tCheck\nA setting seems ignored\tExact key/section spelling, one source block, no inline value comments, and latest patch installed.\nGUI changes disappear on a new run\tAssemble & Emulate reapplies explicit source settings. Edit the source.\nA supposedly disabled device appears\tDisable it explicitly; omitted values can inherit a saved profile.\nVIA2 does not operate the LCD/SD\tVIA2 enabled, 2... pin strings, correct driver base, correct DDR settings.\nLCD is enabled but blank\tLCD initialisation sequence, correct wiring/width, DDR registers and command delays.\nSimple console has no input\tRX enabled at TX+1, simulator running, console focused, code reading RX.\nACIA receives nothing\tCorrect transport/port, TCP client connected or physical Connect pressed, matching ACIA registers and driver base.\nW65C51N loses burst output\tCheck acia_tx_bug; the enabled fault requires software pacing.\nSD shows no card\tExisting image, correct source-relative path, compatible geometry and permissions.\nSD reads work but writes fail\tread_only, image permissions, driver errors and strict timing limits.\nSD initialisation fails only in strict mode\tAt least 74 startup clocks with CS high, CS handling and SPI frequency no greater than the startup limit.\nTwo peripherals interfere\tOverlapping CPU address ranges or shared VIA pins/direction changes.\n\nThis guide was checked against the patched profile parser and GTK application paths. Its device snippets describe configuration; they are not complete driver programs. Hardware-model limitations still apply."},
};
#define PROFILE_GUIDE_COUNT ((int)(sizeof(profile_guide_sections)/sizeof(profile_guide_sections[0])))
